coi la .ilmen.

I just applied your idea (added split entries) and added merged entries... 
And I also found a very simple way to add compound cmavo!
Indeed:

   - I created a script that splits jbovlaste entries into cmavo and 
   non-cmavo, by using a simple regex (using rules listed in the CLL, chapter 
   4.2)
   - Then I tagged all cmavo with a flag "C", and added the Hunspell rule 
   "CCC*" (~= "CC+"), which means you can "glue" 2 or more cmavo together.
   
Of course, this will allow un-grammatical things such as "lonulonucalo", 
but once again this is not the spell-checker role.

I tried your example "calonu". It seems the "lonu" entry exists, so my 
dictionary inteprets that as a "normal word" (= non-simple-cmavo) instead 
of a "compound cmavo". But all following combinations are now valid :

   - ca, lo, nu
   - lo nu, lonu, ca lo, calo
   - ca lonu, calo nu, calonu
   
Only calo & calonu are detected as a compound (remember "lonu" is an 
entry), but anyway that works as expected.
Experimental cmavo support will be added soon.

Do you know other rules that could be great integrating?
Please test ( https://github.com/Sukender/lojban-spell-check-dist ) and 
give feedback! ki'e

I still have issues with dots in LibreOffice (.i .a and such)... And some 
words of "le cmalu noltru" are not recognized yet. Is there any other word 
source I can use?

co'o

-- 
Sukender

